-- Q1: medium / L2 — GASB 96 SBITA asset capitalization
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Maple Ridge enters a 3-year subscription IT arrangement on January 1, 2026, for cloud-based accounting software at $84,000 annually, incurring $12,000 in preliminary evaluation costs and $25,000 in implementation costs. Under GASB Statement No. 96, which costs are included in the subscription asset?', '["Only the present value of future subscription payments", "The present value of future subscription payments plus the $25,000 implementation costs", "The present value of future subscription payments plus all project costs totaling $37,000", "The undiscounted total of subscription payments plus all project costs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 96, the subscription asset includes the initial subscription liability (present value of payments) plus capitalizable initial implementation stage costs. The $25,000 implementation costs are included because they are incurred during the initial implementation stage. The $12,000 preliminary evaluation costs must be expensed as incurred per GASB 96. Choice C incorrectly includes preliminary project stage costs, which the standard requires to be expensed rather than capitalized.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Interfund loan reporting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Greenfield County''s general fund lends $750,000 to its parks and recreation special revenue fund on October 1, 2026, with repayment expected within six months. Under GASB standards, how should the general fund report this transaction at the December 31 fiscal year-end?', '["An expenditure of $750,000 and a reduction in fund balance", "A transfer out of $750,000 to the special revenue fund", "A due from other funds receivable of $750,000", "An investment in the special revenue fund of $750,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, interfund loans are reported as receivables (''due from other funds'') in the lending fund and payables (''due to other funds'') in the borrowing fund when repayment is expected within a reasonable time. The general fund reports a $750,000 receivable because the loan is temporary. Choice B incorrectly classifies the loan as a transfer, which represents a permanent reallocation of resources rather than a temporary lending arrangement requiring repayment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L3 — GASB 87 lease fund vs government-wide
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Cedarville enters a 10-year equipment lease on January 1, 2026, with annual payments of $95,000 due each December 31. The lease liability measured at the incremental borrowing rate totals $712,000. When the general fund makes the first $95,000 payment, how does reporting differ between the governmental fund statements and the government-wide statements?', '["The fund statements report a $95,000 expenditure with no lease liability, while government-wide statements recognize amortization expense, interest expense, and a lease liability reduced by the principal portion of the payment", "Both the fund statements and government-wide statements record the $712,000 lease liability and a $95,000 annual expenditure", "The fund statements record the $712,000 lease liability with a $95,000 expenditure, while government-wide statements record only amortization expense", "The fund statements defer the payment as a prepaid asset, while government-wide statements recognize the full lease liability as an immediate expense"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 87, governmental fund statements use current financial resources and modified accrual accounting, reporting only the $95,000 payment as an expenditure with no long-term liability. Government-wide statements use economic resources and full accrual, recognizing a right-of-use asset amortized over the lease term, a lease liability reduced by principal payments, and separate interest expense. Choice B incorrectly assumes governmental funds report long-term liabilities, violating the current financial resources measurement focus.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Blended vs discrete component unit evaluation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Bayside School District has two legally separate entities: the Bayside School Foundation, whose board is appointed entirely by the school board and which raises funds exclusively for district programs, and the Bayside Transportation Authority, which provides bus services to the district and three neighboring districts under independent governance. Under GASB Statement No. 14 as amended by GASB 61, which presentation is most appropriate?', '["Both entities should be blended into the district''s statements because they provide services to the district", "Both entities should be discretely presented in separate columns because they are legally separate entities", "The Foundation should be discretely presented and the Transportation Authority blended because it provides direct transportation services", "The Foundation should be blended due to its appointed board and exclusive service, while the Transportation Authority should be discretely presented due to independent multi-entity governance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 14 as amended by GASB 61, a component unit is blended when its governing body is substantively the same as the primary government and it provides services almost entirely to the primary government. The Foundation meets both criteria. The Transportation Authority serves multiple districts under independent governance, qualifying only for discrete presentation. Choice B incorrectly applies discrete presentation to both, ignoring the Foundation''s board appointment and exclusive service relationship that require blending.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Deferred outflows of resources definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, which of the following best defines a deferred outflow of resources?', '["An obligation that is due and payable in the current period", "An asset that has been restricted by external parties for capital projects", "A consumption of net assets by the government applicable to a future reporting period", "A reduction in liabilities resulting from current-period operations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, a deferred outflow of resources is a consumption of net assets by the government that is applicable to a future reporting period. Common examples include deferred amounts on debt refundings and certain pension-related items under GASB 68. Choice A describes a current liability rather than a deferred outflow, which specifically relates to resource consumption recognized in future periods.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Special revenue fund definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, what is the defining characteristic of a special revenue fund?', '["It accounts for specific revenue sources restricted or committed for purposes other than debt service or capital projects", "It accounts for all financial resources related to capital acquisitions and improvements", "It accounts for resources held in trust for individuals or organizations outside the government", "It accounts for any revenue source designated by the governing body for general operations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, a special revenue fund accounts for and reports specific revenue sources that are restricted or committed to expenditure for purposes other than debt service or capital projects. The fund must have a substantial committed or restricted revenue source as its foundation. Choice B describes a capital projects fund rather than a special revenue fund, confusing the purpose restrictions that distinguish these two governmental fund types.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Major fund determination criteria
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Pemberton reports total governmental fund assets of $80 million with no enterprise funds. Its capital projects fund has total assets of $9.2 million. The finance director is determining whether this fund qualifies as a major fund. Under GASB Statement No. 34, does the capital projects fund meet the quantitative major fund criteria?', '["No, because capital projects funds must always be reported as nonmajor funds regardless of size", "Yes, because the fund''s assets of $9.2 million exceed both the 10% individual-category threshold and the 5% combined threshold", "No, because the fund must exceed 10% of total government-wide assets, not fund-level assets", "Yes, but only if the government''s chief financial officer specifically designates it as major"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 76, a governmental fund is major if any financial statement element (assets, liabilities, revenues, or expenditures) is at least 10% of the corresponding total for all governmental funds and at least 5% of the combined governmental and enterprise total. The capital projects fund''s $9.2 million represents 11.5% of the $80 million total, meeting both thresholds since there are no enterprise funds. Choice A incorrectly suggests capital projects funds cannot qualify as major.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Required supplementary information
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, which of the following is classified as required supplementary information?', '["The statement of net position for governmental activities", "Notes to the basic financial statements", "The statistical section of the annual comprehensive financial report", "Management''s discussion and analysis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, management''s discussion and analysis (MD&A) is classified as required supplementary information (RSI) that must precede the basic financial statements. Other common RSI includes budgetary comparison schedules and pension or OPEB schedules. Choice C is incorrect because the statistical section is supplementary information that is not required by GASB standards, even though many governments voluntarily include it in their annual comprehensive financial reports.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — OPEB trust vs no trust reporting evaluation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ridgefield provides post-employment health benefits to retired employees with a total OPEB obligation of $28 million. The city council is deciding whether to establish an irrevocable OPEB trust or continue pay-as-you-go funding. The finance director asks how each approach affects government-wide reporting under GASB 75. Which evaluation of the reporting consequences is most accurate?', '["With a trust, the city reports a net OPEB liability reduced by plan net position and recognizes deferred inflows and outflows; without a trust, the city reports the total OPEB liability with no fiduciary net position offset", "With a trust, the city eliminates the OPEB liability entirely from the government-wide statements because the trust assumes the obligation", "Without a trust, the city reports no liability because pay-as-you-go funding means benefits are expensed only when paid", "Both approaches produce identical government-wide liabilities because GASB 75 ignores the funding mechanism"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 75, a government with an irrevocable trust reports a net OPEB liability (total OPEB liability minus fiduciary net position) along with related deferred inflows and outflows. Without a trust, the government reports the full total OPEB liability with no fiduciary net position offset. Choice C incorrectly suggests pay-as-you-go eliminates the liability, but GASB 75 requires accrual-basis recognition of the obligation regardless of funding approach.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — GASB 89 interest capitalization prohibition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Springdale issues $15 million in general obligation bonds in January 2026 to construct a civic center. During the 18-month construction period, the city incurs $420,000 in interest costs. A staff accountant suggests capitalizing the interest. Under GASB Statement No. 89, how does the interest treatment in the city''s government-wide statements differ from a private-sector entity applying ASC 835-20?', '["Both the city and a private-sector entity capitalize the $420,000 as part of the asset cost during construction", "Both the city and a private-sector entity expense the $420,000 in the period incurred", "The city expenses the $420,000, while a private-sector entity applying ASC 835-20 would capitalize construction-period interest as part of the asset cost", "The city defers the $420,000 as a deferred outflow of resources, while a private-sector entity expenses it immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Statement No. 89, state and local governments are prohibited from capitalizing interest costs incurred during the construction period of capital assets. The city must expense the $420,000 when incurred on its government-wide statements. In contrast, ASC 835-20 requires private-sector entities to capitalize qualifying interest during construction as part of the asset''s historical cost. Choice A incorrectly assumes governmental entities follow the same interest capitalization rules as private-sector entities.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Interfund transfer in government-wide statement of activities
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Redstone''s general fund transfers $500,000 to its water utility enterprise fund to subsidize infrastructure repairs during fiscal year 2026. The general fund reports a transfer out and the enterprise fund reports a transfer in. When preparing the government-wide statement of activities, how are these interfund transfers reported?', '["Both the transfer out and transfer in are eliminated, resulting in no impact on either the governmental or business-type activities columns", "The transfer out appears in governmental activities and the transfer in appears in business-type activities, with no net effect on total government-wide change in net position", "The transfer is reclassified as a capital contribution reported only in the business-type activities column", "Both the transfer out and transfer in are reported only in the governmental activities column as an internal reallocation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 112, transfers between governmental and business-type activities are displayed separately in each column of the statement of activities. The $500,000 transfer out reduces governmental activities'' change in net position while the transfer in increases business-type activities'' by the same amount, producing zero net effect at the total government level. Choice A incorrectly assumes transfers between columns are eliminated, but GASB 34 requires them to be displayed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Net investment in capital assets calculation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Havenport reports capital assets of $42 million net of accumulated depreciation, outstanding capital-related debt of $28 million, and unspent bond proceeds restricted for capital construction of $3 million on its government-wide statement of net position. Under GASB Statement No. 34, what is the net investment in capital assets?', '["$17 million", "$14 million", "$42 million", "$24 million"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, net investment in capital assets equals capital assets net of depreciation ($42 million) minus outstanding capital-related debt ($28 million) plus unspent debt proceeds ($3 million), yielding $17 million. The unspent proceeds are added back because those resources have not yet been converted into capital assets. Choice B ($14 million) incorrectly omits the $3 million adjustment for unspent bond proceeds, overstating the net debt attributable to existing assets.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Proprietary fund measurement focus
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'What measurement focus do proprietary funds use under GASB standards?', '["Current financial resources measurement focus", "Modified accrual measurement focus", "Cash basis measurement focus", "Economic resources measurement focus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, proprietary funds (enterprise and internal service funds) use the economic resources measurement focus and full accrual basis of accounting, the same approach used in private-sector accounting. This means all assets, liabilities, revenues, and expenses are reported regardless of when cash is received or paid. Choice A is incorrect because the current financial resources measurement focus applies to governmental funds, not proprietary funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Budgetary comparison variance calculation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Fairhaven adopted a general fund budget with estimated revenues of $18,500,000 and appropriations of $17,800,000. Actual revenues totaled $18,750,000, and actual expenditures were $17,950,000. Under GASB Statement No. 34, what is the variance between budgeted and actual revenues on the budgetary comparison schedule?', '["$150,000 unfavorable", "$700,000 favorable", "$250,000 favorable", "$800,000 favorable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, the budgetary comparison schedule reports the variance between final budgeted and actual amounts for each line item. The revenue variance is actual revenues of $18,750,000 minus budgeted revenues of $18,500,000, yielding a $250,000 favorable variance because actual collections exceeded the estimate. Choice A ($150,000 unfavorable) incorrectly reports the expenditure variance ($17,950,000 minus $17,800,000) rather than the revenue variance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Tax abatement disclosure evaluation (GASB 77)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Thorndale enters a property tax abatement with Pinnacle Manufacturing, reducing taxes by $1.2 million annually for 10 years to incentivize a new factory. Two neighboring counties entered similar abatements affecting the city''s tax base. The finance director is uncertain whether to report the abatement as a revenue reduction or an expenditure and whether to disclose the other governments'' abatements. Which approach best satisfies GASB Statement No. 77?', '["Report the abatement as an expenditure of $1.2 million and disclose only the city''s own agreement", "Disclose the abatement program purpose, the $1.2 million annual reduction and its impact on revenue, and other governments'' abatements that reduce the city''s tax revenue", "Report the $12 million total abatement as a long-term liability with no annual disclosure", "Disclose only that a tax abatement agreement exists without specifying dollar amounts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 77, governments must disclose tax abatement agreements including the program description, the gross dollar amount of taxes abated, and any commitments. The standard also requires disclosure of other governments'' tax abatements that reduce the reporting government''s revenue. GASB 77 treats abatements as revenue reductions, not expenditures. Choice A incorrectly classifies the abatement as an expenditure and ignores the requirement to disclose other governments'' abatements affecting the city.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Government-mandated nonexchange transaction timing
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On July 1, 2026, the state mandates that all counties provide after-school tutoring beginning September 1 and appropriates $4 million to Millbrook County. The state transfers $2.5 million on August 15 and the remaining $1.5 million on February 1, 2027. The county''s fiscal year ends December 31 with a 60-day availability period. Under GASB Statement No. 33 and modified accrual, how much revenue should the county recognize in fiscal year 2026?', '["$4 million on July 1, 2026, when the legislature mandates the program", "$2.5 million on August 15, 2026, when the first cash payment is received", "$4 million on September 1, 2026, when the program eligibility period begins", "$2.5 million, because the $1.5 million received February 1, 2027, falls outside the 60-day availability period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 33, government-mandated nonexchange transactions are recognized when eligibility requirements are met and, for governmental funds, when resources are available. The program begins September 1, meeting eligibility, but the $1.5 million received February 1, 2027, falls outside the 60-day availability window ending February 28. Only $2.5 million meets both criteria. Choice C incorrectly recognizes the full $4 million, ignoring the modified accrual availability criterion that defers the $1.5 million.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Fund balance restricted for unspent bond proceeds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Clarkson issues $8 million in general obligation bonds for a new public library. By fiscal year-end, the city has spent $5.2 million on construction with $2.8 million remaining in the capital projects fund. Under GASB Statement No. 54, how should the $2.8 million of unspent bond proceeds be classified in fund balance?', '["Restricted fund balance, because bond covenants legally limit the use of proceeds to library construction", "Committed fund balance, because the city council authorized the project by resolution", "Assigned fund balance, because management intends to spend the proceeds on the library", "Unassigned fund balance, because the proceeds have not yet been expended"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, fund balance is classified as restricted when constraints are imposed by external parties such as creditors, grantors, or laws. General obligation bond covenants and enabling legislation legally restrict the use of proceeds to the specified capital purpose. The $2.8 million is restricted because the constraint originates from an external source. Choice B incorrectly classifies the balance as committed, which applies when the governing body imposes constraints by its own formal action rather than external legal requirements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — MD&A purpose
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, what is the primary purpose of management''s discussion and analysis?', '["To present the government''s compliance with federal grant requirements", "To provide an objective and easily readable analysis of the government''s financial activities based on currently known facts", "To display budget-to-actual comparisons for all governmental funds", "To list all accounting policies used in preparing the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 8, management''s discussion and analysis (MD&A) provides an objective, easily readable analysis of the government''s financial activities based on currently known facts, decisions, or conditions. It serves as a narrative introduction to the basic financial statements. Choice D describes the notes to the financial statements, as the summary of significant accounting policies is a required note component, not part of MD&A.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Bond premium treatment fund vs government-wide
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Westbrook issues $10 million in general obligation bonds at 103. The $300,000 premium is transferred to the debt service fund per bond covenants. In the first year, the debt service fund uses $150,000 of the premium for interest payments. How does treatment of the bond premium differ between the debt service fund and the government-wide statements?', '["Both the debt service fund and government-wide statements amortize the premium over the bond term using the effective interest method", "The debt service fund records the premium as a direct reduction of bonds payable, while government-wide statements recognize it as revenue", "The debt service fund reports the premium as an other financing source and the interest payment as an expenditure, while government-wide statements add the premium to the bonds payable carrying amount and amortize it over the term", "The debt service fund capitalizes the premium as a long-term asset, while government-wide statements expense it immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, the debt service fund reports bond premiums received as other financing sources and records interest payments as expenditures using modified accrual. Government-wide statements add the $300,000 premium to the bonds payable carrying amount ($10.3 million) and amortize it as a reduction of interest expense over the bond term. Choice A incorrectly assumes governmental funds use accrual-based premium amortization, but funds recognize only current financial resources.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Pension contribution vs expense difference
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Granville contributes $3.2 million to its single-employer defined benefit pension plan during fiscal year 2026. The actuarially determined pension expense is $4.1 million, reflecting service cost, interest, and deferred item changes. A council member assumes the city''s pension expense equals its cash contribution. Under GASB Statement No. 68, how does the $900,000 difference affect the government-wide financial statements?', '["The $900,000 is reported as a reduction in restricted net position", "The excess expense is eliminated through an adjustment to deferred outflows of resources", "The government-wide statements report only the $3.2 million contribution as pension expense", "The government-wide statements report the full $4.1 million pension expense, and the $900,000 difference increases the net pension liability"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 68, pension expense on government-wide statements is actuarially determined and often differs from the cash contribution. When the contribution ($3.2 million) is less than recognized expense ($4.1 million), the net pension liability increases by the $900,000 difference because the government has not fully funded the cost recognized in the current period. Choice C incorrectly equates pension expense with cash contributions, but GASB 68 requires accrual-based expense recognition.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Pass-through grant analysis
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The State of Newland receives a $6 million federal grant for disaster relief and passes $4.5 million to Benton County while retaining $1.5 million for state-level coordination. The state has administrative involvement in monitoring Benton County''s use of funds. Under GASB Statement No. 24, how should the state report the $4.5 million passed through to Benton County?', '["The state reports $4.5 million as both revenue and expenditure because it has administrative involvement, resulting in gross reporting", "The state reports only the $1.5 million it retains as revenue and excludes the pass-through from its financial statements", "The state reports the full $6 million as revenue and the $4.5 million as a contra-revenue reduction", "The state reports the $4.5 million as a liability to Benton County until eligible costs are incurred"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 24, when a government serves as a pass-through entity with administrative involvement or discretion over fund distribution, it must report the pass-through amount as both revenue and expenditure in its financial statements. The state recognizes $4.5 million in federal grant revenue and $4.5 million in grant expenditures, resulting in gross reporting. Choice B incorrectly excludes the pass-through, which is appropriate only when the state acts as a mere cash conduit with no administrative involvement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — Fines and penalties classification (GASB 33)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Fairview collects $185,000 in building code violation fines during fiscal year 2026. Under GASB Statement No. 33, how should these fines be classified for nonexchange revenue recognition?', '["Derived tax revenues because they result from assessments on underlying exchange transactions", "Imposed nonexchange revenues because they are assessed by the government on nongovernmental entities", "Government-mandated nonexchange transactions because the government requires compliance with building codes", "Voluntary nonexchange transactions because violators could have avoided the fines"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 33, fines and penalties are classified as imposed nonexchange revenues. Imposed nonexchange revenues are assessments by governments on nongovernmental entities, including property taxes, fines, and forfeitures, that are not derived from underlying exchange transactions. Choice A is incorrect because derived tax revenues like sales and income taxes result from assessments on exchange transactions, whereas building code fines are imposed without an underlying exchange.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Prior-period adjustment in government-wide statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'During fiscal year 2026, the City of Belmont discovers it failed to record $1.4 million of accumulated depreciation on infrastructure assets in its government-wide statements for fiscal years 2023 and 2024. Under GASB Statement No. 62, how does this correction affect the government-wide financial statements?', '["The $1.4 million is recorded as additional depreciation expense in the 2026 statement of activities", "The $1.4 million is reported as a special item in the 2026 statement of activities below operating expenses", "The $1.4 million is disclosed only in the notes with no adjustment to reported net position", "Beginning net position of the earliest period presented is restated downward by $1.4 million, and comparative statements are revised if presented"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 62, paragraphs 81-82, corrections of errors in previously issued financial statements require restatement of the beginning net position of the earliest period presented and revision of any comparative-period statements displayed. The $1.4 million adjustment flows through beginning net position, not current-year expense. Choice A incorrectly records the catch-up depreciation as current-period expense, which overstates 2026 expenses and fails to correct the periods where the error originated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — TIF fund type recommendation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Oak Harbor creates a tax increment financing district to revitalize downtown. The TIF will capture incremental property tax growth over 20 years to repay $12 million in bonds issued for public infrastructure. Incremental revenues are pledged solely to bond repayment, and the city retains risk if revenues fall short. The finance director must determine the appropriate fund types. Which recommendation best reflects GASB guidance?', '["Report all TIF activity in the general fund because the city retains the credit risk on the bonds", "Report all TIF activity in a single enterprise fund because the district is self-supporting from pledged revenues", "Report bond proceeds and construction in a capital projects fund and debt service payments from incremental tax revenue in a debt service fund", "Report TIF activity in a private-purpose trust fund because the benefits flow primarily to private property owners"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB standards, TIF arrangements involving governmental debt and tax-funded repayment use governmental fund types. Bond proceeds and construction spending are reported in a capital projects fund, while incremental property tax revenues pledged for bond repayment are reported in a debt service fund. This dual-fund approach matches the economic substance of the transactions. Choice B incorrectly classifies the TIF as an enterprise fund, which is reserved for activities financed primarily through user charges rather than tax revenues.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Investment trust fund definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 84, what is the purpose of an investment trust fund?', '["To report the external portion of investment pools managed by the sponsoring government on behalf of other entities", "To account for a government''s own investment portfolio and track unrealized gains and losses", "To hold bond proceeds pending disbursement for capital construction projects", "To account for pension contributions invested for future employee benefit payments"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 84, an investment trust fund reports the external portion of investment pools operated by the sponsoring government for legally separate entities that participate in the pool. Only the external participants'' share is reported in the trust fund; the sponsoring government''s own share is in its participating fund. Choice D describes a pension trust fund, not an investment trust fund, which specifically serves external participants in government-operated investment pools.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Infrastructure modified approach condition assessment
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Granite County reports its 2,400-mile road network using the modified approach under GASB Statement No. 34. The latest condition assessment shows 18% of roads below the established condition level versus a policy target of 85% at acceptable condition. Preservation spending was $6.8 million against an estimated $9.2 million needed. The finance director must evaluate whether the county can continue the modified approach. Which assessment is most accurate?', '["The county can continue the modified approach because the 18% failure rate is an acceptable temporary variance", "The county must evaluate whether conditions have deteriorated below the established level and whether preservation spending is adequate, potentially requiring a switch to depreciation-based reporting", "The county automatically loses eligibility for the modified approach whenever preservation spending falls below the estimated amount in any single year", "The county should split reporting, using the modified approach for roads meeting the standard and depreciation for roads below condition level"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraphs 23-25, the modified approach requires governments to maintain infrastructure at or above an established condition level and perform assessments at least every three years. When conditions deteriorate and preservation spending is insufficient, the government must evaluate whether it can continue meeting modified approach requirements or must switch to depreciation-based reporting. Choice C incorrectly applies an automatic disqualification trigger, but GASB 34 requires an overall evaluation rather than a single-year test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Derived tax revenue recognition timing
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On December 31, 2026, the State of Newland has collected $48 million in sales tax through November. December sales tax of $5.2 million is received January 28, 2027, within the state''s 60-day availability period. Under GASB Statement No. 33 and modified accrual, how much total sales tax revenue should the general fund recognize for fiscal year 2026?', '["$48 million, because derived tax revenues require cash receipt before year-end", "$48 million, with the $5.2 million recognized in 2027 because the January receipt relates to a different fiscal year", "The full $53.2 million, because December sales generated the tax regardless of when cash is received", "$53.2 million, because the underlying December transactions occurred during the fiscal year and the January receipt falls within the 60-day availability period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 33, derived tax revenues such as sales taxes are recognized in the period of the underlying exchange transaction. Under modified accrual, revenue must also be available, meaning collected within the availability period. The December 2026 sales generated the $5.2 million, and the January 28 receipt falls within 60 days, so the full $53.2 million is recognized in 2026. Choice A incorrectly requires cash receipt before year-end, ignoring the availability period extension.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Special assessment debt not obligated
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Ridgewood levies a $2 million special assessment on property owners to finance new sidewalks and issues $2 million in special assessment bonds, pledging the levies as the primary repayment source. The town is not obligated in any manner for the debt. Under GASB standards, how should the town report the special assessment debt?', '["As general long-term debt in the government-wide statement of net position", "As a liability in the debt service fund with a corresponding receivable from property owners", "The debt is not reported as a town liability; it is disclosed in the notes to the financial statements", "As a contingent liability in the government-wide statements with a probable loss accrual"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB standards (Codification Section S40), when a government is not obligated in any manner for special assessment debt, the debt is not reported as a liability in the government''s financial statements. Instead, the government discloses the outstanding debt and the fact that it bears no obligation in the notes. Choice A incorrectly reports the debt as a governmental liability, but GASB requires only debt for which the government is obligated to be recognized as a liability.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Private-purpose trust fund definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 84, what type of activity is reported in a private-purpose trust fund?', '["Trust arrangements where both the principal and income benefit individuals, private organizations, or other governments", "Resources held for the government''s own employees'' pension benefits", "Investment pools managed on behalf of external participants in the sponsoring government''s pool", "Taxes and fees collected on behalf of another government pending distribution"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB Statement No. 84, a private-purpose trust fund reports trust arrangements in which the principal and income benefit individuals, private organizations, or other governments, such as escheat property or scholarship trusts for private beneficiaries. Choice B describes a pension trust fund, which is a separate fiduciary fund type specifically for employee benefit plan assets rather than the broad private-purpose category.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Committed fund balance definition (GASB 54)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, what distinguishes committed fund balance from other fund balance classifications?', '["Constraints are imposed by external creditors or grantors through legal requirements", "Resources can only be used for debt service principal and interest payments", "Management has expressed an intention to use funds for a specific purpose without formal action", "Constraints are imposed by the government''s highest level of decision-making authority through formal action"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB Statement No. 54, committed fund balance represents resources constrained by the government''s highest level of decision-making authority (typically the governing body) through formal action such as a resolution or ordinance. These constraints can only be removed by the same authority through equivalent formal action. Choice A describes restricted fund balance, which involves externally imposed constraints from creditors, grantors, or constitutional provisions rather than the government''s own formal decisions.', 'easy', 1)

;

COMMIT;
